Compare three rounds of AUTH CPU-time measurements. Interleave short blocks
and alternate their order, preserving 3*n requests per case and the original
30% regression limit. Fixed-order full blocks failed by 40.8% on Linux;
the same operation varied 2.7-fold between blocks, without matching GC cost.
Sums over the interleaved blocks still failed in full runs (SX25519/SX25519
in the seed 803636663 run): a few blocks absorb whole-process CPU noise and
the sum weights every block equally. Compare the median of the per-pair
relative differences instead. Each adjacent wrong-key/missing-queue block
pair is measured back-to-back, so shared noise cancels within a pair, while
a systematic timing leak shifts most pairs and still exceeds the limit.
Print the sum difference as well for diagnosis. Do not discard slow samples
or retry until the timing assertion passes.

Generate one unrestricted TLS credential for each notification service test
and reuse it across connections and server restarts.  tls 2.4 rejects the
server-only fixture for client authentication, while generating a new
credential for every connection changes the SMP service identity.

Read CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID through System.Clock instead of timeit's
System.CPUTime. GHC 9.10 selects getrusage on OpenBSD (_POSIX_TIMERS == -1);
that backend reports sampled ticks, quantizing these short blocks to 10 ms.
OpenBSD's process CPU clock uses accumulated runtime instead. Keep the CPU
metric, all samples, aggregation, response assertions and threshold. Reject
nonpositive samples explicitly rather than passing NaN to the comparison.
Wall time and optional +RTS -T GC statistics remain diagnostics only.

Read service-subscription metrics strictly. The lazy reader leaves the
handle locked while the periodic writer reopens it; its resource-busy
exception terminates the server and makes the client fail with TLS EOF.

